diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the simple_bgp topology found no fault at any layer. Phase 1 reachability confirmed 0% loss in both directions between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2). Phase A swept all four devices for ACL rules, addressing anomalies, static ARP poisoning, and MAC conflicts — all clean. Phase B ran the full BGP snapshot: both FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd) are healthy on router1 and router2; the eBGP session between them (193.10.11.1 ↔ 193.10.11.2) is Established with 1 connection established and 0 dropped; router2 (AS 2) is correctly advertising 200.1.1.0/24 and router1 (AS 1) has that prefix installed as B>* in its FIB. The running configs contain no route-maps, prefix-lists, or neighbor filters of any kind. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress tools and no resource anomalies. Examination of /var/log/frr/frr.log on both routers — where debug BGP update logging is active — shows a single clean initial UPDATE exchange at lab start, routine AS-path loop denials (normal BGP behaviour), and then only keepalive traffic every 60 seconds. There are zero route withdrawals and zero session resets in the entire log history. The user's claim that pc2's prefix "went missing" from router1's routing table is directly contradicted by every observable artefact: the route is present, the session is stable, traffic flows, and the logs carry no trace of a prior withdrawal event. Root cause: no anomaly exists — the BGP fabric is operating correctly and the reported symptom cannot be confirmed from current state or historical evidence. Fix: no corrective action is required; the network is healthy as-is.
